      "comment": "Viewing this film so soon after The Conformist and The Garden of the Finzi-Continis makes comparisons irresistible. Like the two earlier films, this one features a lush, \"retro\" mise-en-scene. But it is much more rapidly cut, with a heavy use of eyeline cues to stitch together the changes from shot to shot. Another way to put this is to say that Life is Beautiful, with its pattern of actions and reactions, exemplifies what Deleuze had called the action-image, whereas the earlier films, especially The Conformist, unfold in the manner of the time-image, whereby the cutting may occasionally disconnect itself from character psychology. All the same, Life Is Beautiful certainly exhibits some peculiarities in structure. Its first 48 minutes work almost as a separate film, with its own unique narrative arc, concerned with Guido and Dora's courtship, prior to the birth of Joshua. But while doing the cinemetrics count, I didn't detect any difference in editing between the film's first and second halves. I did, however, notice that the final scenes in the camp between Guido and his son featured some unusually long takes, as if the director wanted, at this point in the story, to keep these two characters within the same frame. 0.00: \"Arrezo 1939\"; the brakes on the car fail, causing Guido and Ferruccio to careen out of control, and crash by the roadside, near a facist parade; 25.00: Guido and Dora attend an Offenbach operetta set in Venice; 48.30: flashforward to the married life of Guido and Dora, and their six-year old son Giosue, a.k.a. Joshua; 61.30: the train arrives in the concentration camp, and the prisoners, including Guido and his family, disembark.",
